1. INTRODUCTION
Digital payment systems and networked computing infrastructures
generate large volumes of behavioural data. Hidden within this data are
rare but high-impact events such as fraudulent transactions and
malicious network connections. These events are difficult to detect
using static rule-based systems because fraud patterns and attack
behaviours evolve continuously.
Machine learning offers a practical way to model complex, non-linear
patterns in such data. However, many academic anomaly detection
projects remain limited to isolated experiments. They often lack a
reusable preprocessing pipeline, an inference API, user-facing
dashboards, and interpretable predictions. This project addresses that
gap by building a unified anomaly detection platform with separate
domain modules and shared engineering practices.
1.1 Objectives
● Design a modular anomaly detection platform for fraud
and intrusion detection.
● Build an IEEE-CIS fraud preprocessing pipeline that handles
missing values, categorical variables, and numerical scaling.
● Train and evaluate Logistic Regression, XGBoost, Random
Forest, and Isolation Forest models where appropriate.
● Expose model inference through FastAPI endpoints for fraud and
intrusion prediction.
● Provide an interactive Streamlit dashboard for demonstration
and user interaction.
● Document performance, limitations, and future improvements in
a reproducible manner.

2. LITERATURE REVIEW
2.1 Fraud Detection Research
Fraud detection research has traditionally used highly imbalanced
transaction datasets where fraudulent examples represent only a small
fraction of all records. Earlier work commonly used Logistic Regression,
Random Forest, gradient boosting, and synthetic oversampling
techniques. Recent fraud detection systems increasingly use richer
transaction metadata, categorical identity information, and tree-based
boosting models.
2.2 Network Intrusion Detection Research
Network intrusion detection research has used benchmark datasets such
as KDD Cup 1999 and NSL-KDD to compare classical machine
learning models. Random Forest, Support Vector Machines, neural
networks, and XGBoost have all shown strong results. A continuing
challenge in both domains is the transition from model accuracy to
operational usefulness, including deployment, monitoring, and
explainability.

3. SYSTEM DESIGN AND ARCHITECTURE
The platform follows a modular architecture. Domain-specific code is
separated into fraud and intrusion modules, while shared preprocessing
utilities and common model-serving logic are reused where possible.
The backend API is built with FastAPI, and the frontend dashboard is
built with Streamlit.
The fraud module includes data loading, preprocessing, model training,
anomaly scoring, and explainability utilities. The API loads trained

model artifacts from the models directory and accepts raw IEEE-CIS-
style records for inference. The dashboard communicates with the API

instead of loading models directly for primary prediction workflows.

4. DATASETS
4.1 IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection Dataset
The updated fraud component uses the IEEE-CIS Fraud Detection
dataset. The main training files are train_transaction.csv and
train_identity.csv. Transaction data contains monetary, product, card,
address, matching, count, time-delta, and V-series engineered features.
Identity data provides device and identity-related attributes. These files
are joined on TransactionID.
4.2 NSL-KDD Dataset
The intrusion detection component uses the NSL-KDD dataset, a
cleaned version of KDD Cup 1999. It contains network connection
features and labels indicating normal or attack traffic. For this platform,
the problem is modelled as binary classification: normal traffic versus
attack traffic.

5. DATA PREPROCESSING
The IEEE-CIS fraud preprocessing pipeline detects the target column
isFraud, drops non-predictive identifiers from the feature matrix, splits
data using stratification, imputes missing numerical values with
medians, imputes missing categorical values with the most frequent
category, applies one-hot encoding with unknown-category handling,
and scales numerical columns.
Unlike the previous ULB credit card dataset version, the IEEE-CIS
dataset is not limited to PCA-transformed V1–V28 features and
Amount. It includes many categorical and sparse identity features, so a
saved preprocessing pipeline is required at inference time. The artifact
models/fraud/fraud_preprocessor.pkl is therefore part of the deployed
fraud system.

6. MODEL DEVELOPMENT
6.1 Fraud Detection
Logistic Regression is retained as a baseline and XGBoost is used as
the primary supervised model. XGBoost is suitable for tabular data,
handles non-linear interactions effectively, and provides strong
performance on imbalanced classification tasks. The model uses
scale_pos_weight to account for class imbalance without synthetically
expanding the transformed feature matrix.
Isolation Forest is trained as an unsupervised anomaly detection layer.
Its score is combined with the XGBoost fraud probability to produce
a combined risk score, weighting the two signals to produce a final
risk- level assessment.
6.2 Intrusion Detection
For intrusion detection, Random Forest and XGBoost models are used to
classify network records as normal or attack. Both models are evaluated
on the NSL-KDD test set to provide comparative performance metrics.

7. EXPLAINABILITY
SHAP explainability utilities are included to provide global and local
interpretation of tree-based models. SHAP helps identify which features
most strongly influence model predictions and supports model
auditability. In the dashboard, prediction outputs include probability and
risk-level summaries; additional SHAP plots can be regenerated
through the explainability scripts.

8. SYSTEM IMPLEMENTATION
8.1 FastAPI Backend
The FastAPI backend exposes three key endpoints for fraud prediction,
intrusion prediction, and health checking. The fraud endpoint accepts
either a single raw IEEE-CIS record or a list of records. Before
prediction, the saved fraud preprocessor aligns incoming records to the
training feature schema. All risk levels are classified as: ≥0.8 → HIGH,
≥0.5 → MEDIUM, ≥0.3 → LOW, <0.3 → MINIMAL.

