11. LIMITATIONS AND FUTURE WORK
11.1 Current Limitations
● The dashboard batch workflow sends rows sequentially, which is
slow for very large CSV files.
● Training was performed on 200,000 rows; full IEEE-CIS dataset
training on higher-resource hardware would likely improve
metrics further.
● The FastAPI backend runs locally; it is not yet cloud-deployed
for full accessibility.
11.2 Future Work
● Add vectorised batch prediction, background jobs, and
downloadable result files for large CSV uploads.
● Hyperparameter tuning via grid search or Bayesian
optimization for both fraud and intrusion models.
● Docker packaging and CI/CD pipeline via GitHub Actions
for automated testing and model validation.
● Deploy FastAPI backend to Render or Railway for full cloud
accessibility.
● Add model drift monitoring using Evidently AI to
detect distribution shift from training data.
● Extend intrusion detection to multi-class classification (Normal /
DoS / Probe / R2L / U2R).

12. CONCLUSION
The updated project successfully demonstrates an end-to-end anomaly
detection platform that supports two important security domains. The
migration to the IEEE-CIS fraud dataset makes the fraud module more
realistic and technically robust because it now handles categorical,
numerical, sparse, and identity features across joined transaction and
identity tables.
The combination of preprocessing, supervised learning, anomaly
scoring, API serving, and dashboard interaction reflects a complete
machine learning engineering workflow. The platform achieves
competitive model performance (XGBoost ROC-AUC 0.9411 on fraud,
0.9696 on intrusion detection), provides SHAP explainability, exposes a
documented REST API, and is accessible via a live web dashboard —
all within a single, modular codebase.

